1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:23,800
HOST: In 1763, Britain won the biggest war of the century, and went broke doing it. The national debt had doubled. The empire was larger than ever, and the treasury was empty.  So Parliament looked across the Atlantic, at thirteen colonies that had paid little toward their own defense. The colonists would help pay. What could go wrong?

2
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:40,400
HOST: By the end of this lesson, three things. One: virtual versus actual representation, and why it mattered. Two: the escalation from the Sugar Act to the Tea Act. Three: how British coercion after the Tea Party unified the colonies.

3
00:00:40,400 --> 00:01:09,800
HOST: For decades, Britain had practiced what historians call salutary neglect, salutary meaning healthy. London passed trade laws and looked the other way, while the colonies ran their own affairs and taxed themselves through their own assemblies. They got used to it.  Then 1763 changed everything. A royal proclamation drew a line down the Appalachian Mountains: no colonial settlement west of here. The war was over, and the old arrangement was finished.

4
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:42,800
HOST: Here is the constitutional question at the center of everything. The one this whole lesson is named for. Parliament said the colonists were virtually represented. Most people in Britain couldn't vote either, but Parliament spoke for the entire empire, so it could tax the entire empire.  The colonists answered: no. Only our own elected assemblies can tax us, because only they answer to us. Actual representation, or nothing. This stopped being about money. It became about who gets to rule.

5
00:01:42,800 --> 00:02:21,400
HOST: The test came fast. The Sugar Act of 1764, the first law meant to raise colonial revenue, not just regulate trade. Then the Stamp Act of 1765: a direct tax on nearly every piece of paper. Legal documents. Newspapers. Pamphlets. Even playing cards and dice.  The colonies exploded. The Sons of Liberty formed. Nine colonies sent delegates to the Stamp Act Congress. Parliament caved and repealed the Stamp Act in 1766.  And on the same day, passed the Declaratory Act, claiming power in all cases whatsoever. They surrendered the tax and kept the principle.

6
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:55,600
HOST: In 1767 came the Townshend Acts, duties on glass, lead, paint, paper, and tea, plus new customs boards and juryless courts to enforce them. The boycotts returned sharper. The Daughters of Liberty spun homespun cloth so families could refuse British textiles. Parliament repealed most of the duties in 1770. But kept the tax on tea, to prove the point.  That same year, in March, British soldiers fired into a Boston crowd. Five colonists died. Paul Revere's engraving turned the clash into propaganda overnight.

7
00:02:55,600 --> 00:03:26,200
HOST: Now the choice. In 1773, Parliament passed the Tea Act, giving the British East India Company a monopoly on colonial tea sales. Here is the twist: the tea got cheaper.  Cheaper. And the colonists destroyed it anyway. Buying it meant accepting Parliament's right to tax them. So on the night of December 16, 1773, the Sons of Liberty, some disguised as Mohawks, dumped 342 chests of tea into Boston Harbor. Principle had beaten price.

8
00:03:26,200 --> 00:04:00,400
HOST: Parliament answered with the Coercive Acts. The colonists called them the Intolerable Acts. Boston's harbor was closed until the tea was paid for, and Massachusetts lost its self-government. The plan was to isolate the radicals.  It did the opposite. Twelve colonies sent delegates to the First Continental Congress. Philadelphia, September 1774. Remember the chain: war debt, then taxes, then resistance, then harsher measures, then wider resistance. That spiral is the road to Lexington and Concord, and the pattern to carry into the Revolution.
